WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.250 --> 00:00:14.670
The average student loan borrower graduates
with around $37,000 in debt and a monthly

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:20.509
payment of almost $400. Considering that the
average starting salary for a college graduate

00:00:20.509 --> 00:00:26.430
is around $50,000, that means that many recent
grads will have to fork over more than 10%

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:31.080
of their paycheck for years to manage their
college loans. That’s quite a burden to

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.559
be carrying at the start of one’s journey.

00:00:33.559 --> 00:00:38.050
It’s no wonder, then, that many young people
faced with this prospect are looking for alternate

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:42.510
ways to prepare for their future, and one
such option is trade school, otherwise known

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:47.940
as vocational school, or CTE. The purpose
of these programs is to train you for a very

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:52.651
specific career, and they tend to be shorter
(and cheaper) than a four-year degree, so

00:00:52.651 --> 00:00:56.289
you can get into the workforce quickly and
start making money.

00:00:56.289 --> 00:01:00.470
If trade school is something you’re considering,
you might be wondering what kind of training

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:05.530
will offer the best bang for your buck. Of
course, salaries can fluctuate based on time,

00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:10.310
region, and your level of experience, but
here’s a rough countdown of ten of the most

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:14.760
lucrative trades that you could train for
in two years or less:

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:18.930
10. Heavy Equipment Operator
9. HVAC Technician

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:21.470
8. Paralegal
7. Plumber

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:22.890
6. Electrician

00:01:22.890 --> 00:01:27.170
5. Geological and Petroleum Technician
4. Sonographer

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:32.460
3. Dental Hygienist
2. Elevator Installer/Repairer

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:35.430
and
1. Radiation Therapist

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:40.380
Keep in mind, these are median salaries, meaning
half the workers in these fields make more,

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:44.850
and half make less. So while you probably
won’t make this much right out of trade

00:01:44.850 --> 00:01:49.500
school, there is potential to earn an even
higher salary someday. Because of health and

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:55.290
safety demands, many of these careers require
licensure, or apprenticeships, or both. And

00:01:55.290 --> 00:02:00.119
while some employers might prefer a bachelor’s
degree, all of these fields should be accessible

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:03.100
to someone with just a two-year associate's
degree.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:08.140
Salary isn’t the only factor that makes
these careers attractive.The “job growth”

00:02:08.149 --> 00:02:13.580
percentage represents how much employment
is expected to increase by 2026--in other

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:17.890
words, how much the demand for workers in
each field will rise--and you can see that

00:02:17.890 --> 00:02:23.860
most of them are well above the general employment
growth rate of 7%. This might be because these

00:02:23.860 --> 00:02:29.980
jobs are more difficult to outsource than,
say, computer programming or manufacturing.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:34.050
Office buildings and homes will always need
plumbing and air conditioning, and you can’t

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:37.820
send a patient overseas for a sonogram or
a teeth cleaning.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:42.651
This might sound promising, but there are
some important caveats. First of all, many

00:02:42.651 --> 00:02:47.810
trade schools are for-profit, so they’re
incentivized to use aggressive recruiting

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:54.080
strategies, including exaggerating their results.
In 2015, one of the largest such companies,

00:02:54.080 --> 00:03:00.080
Corinthian Colleges, was fined $30 million
and forced to close its 28 schools because

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:05.489
it was using fraudulent data to promise unrealistic
employment prospects to potential students.

00:03:05.489 --> 00:03:10.680
And because these schools often target low-income
students whose loans are backed by the federal

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:15.220
government, there’s no incentive for them
to keep tuition low and no consequences for

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:20.459
poor results. This has contributed greatly
to the national student debt crisis, with

00:03:20.459 --> 00:03:26.550
over 40% of for-profit college students eventually
defaulting on their loans.

00:03:26.550 --> 00:03:31.860
Even if you find a reputable, affordable vocational
school, you should be aware of the trade-off.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:36.540
That training may allow you to make more money
right after graduation, but in the long run,

00:03:36.540 --> 00:03:41.849
a 4-year bachelor’s degree will eventually
earn a higher salary. This is partly because

00:03:41.849 --> 00:03:47.299
the modern job market tends to reward flexibility.
It’s estimated that the average American

00:03:47.299 --> 00:03:53.890
changes careers between 3 and 7 times in their
lifetime. Demographic and technological changes

00:03:53.890 --> 00:03:58.590
can reduce demand for some jobs while increasing
others, and someone who’s trained in only

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:03.790
one very specialized skill will have more
trouble adapting than someone with a more

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:05.730
rounded education.

00:04:05.730 --> 00:04:09.670
There are other benefits to a 4-year college
education that are harder to quantify, like

00:04:09.670 --> 00:04:15.739
leadership, communication and analytical skills.
If after 20 years of fixing pipes, Pete decides

00:04:15.739 --> 00:04:20.310
to start his own plumbing business, he’s
going to have to contend with economic theory,

00:04:20.310 --> 00:04:24.460
social demographics, employee management,
client relationships, marketing and design…

00:04:24.460 --> 00:04:29.050
He’ll be trading in his tool belt for a
whole new set of skills that a strictly mechanical

00:04:29.050 --> 00:04:31.440
education might not have prepared him for.

00:04:31.440 --> 00:04:36.750
There is a movement to make vocational training
more holistic, to include more extensive classes

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:42.350
on things like history, social sciences and
writing, so vocational students not only prepare

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:47.680
for a career, but understand how that career
fits into the overall economy, and how it

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:52.060
might change in the future. Unfortunately,
many people still see trade school as nothing

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:57.381
more than an alternative for people not “smart”
enough to attend 4-year college. Vocational

00:04:57.381 --> 00:05:02.100
teachers in high schools often complain that
their classes are treated as a place to stick

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:03.490
“problem students.”

00:05:03.490 --> 00:05:08.320
In fact, there are many reasons someone might
opt for CTE that have nothing to do with academic

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:13.660
capabilities. It might be simply unfeasible
to spend 4 years at an expensive university,

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:18.520
followed by lengthy internships before one
can even start to make an entry-level salary.

00:05:18.520 --> 00:05:23.229
And a lot of people who seek vocational training
genuinely love their work. Many of the trades

00:05:23.229 --> 00:05:27.020
on this list report higher than average job
satisfaction ratings.

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:32.620
Like any education, you should research vocational
schools before enrolling, and speak directly

00:05:32.620 --> 00:05:37.360
to graduates about their experience. And ask
yourself honestly whether you’d be happy

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:39.970
practicing this one trade for the rest of
your life.

00:05:39.970 --> 00:05:44.259
If the answer is no, you should think about
supplementing your training with more general

00:05:44.259 --> 00:05:48.590
academics, perhaps at a local community college,
so you’ll have the flexibility to shift

00:05:48.590 --> 00:05:50.240
gears in the future.

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:51.560
And that’s our two cents!

